In the left panel of Fig.~\ref{fig:ERM_vs_BOE}, we study the MSE loss with $\mathrm{Erf}$ activation in the presence of noise in the teacher labels. We consider the identity outer function, $f(\cdot)=\cdot$, so the teacher noise is additive Gaussian with variance $\Delta^\star$.  By setting $\beta^{-1}=\Delta^\star$, the student correctly accounts for the teacher noise. Additionally, fixing the regularization by setting $\beta\lambda=1$ corresponds to matching the teacher's prior with the one induced by the student $L_2$-regularization in the Gibbs formulation. Under these matched conditions, the Gibbs generalization error must be exactly twice the Bayes-optimal error, as shown in the left panel of Fig.~\ref{fig:ERM_vs_BOE}, for $\beta^{-1}=\Delta^{\star}=10^{-4}$ and $\lambda=10^{-4}$ (blue line). This factor two agrees with the theoretical prediction by Aubin et al. \cite{aubin_committee_2018} for $\mathcal{C}^2$ activations like $\mathrm{Erf}$. In this case, the specialization transitions occurs at $\Tilde{\alpha}_{\rm spec} =\Tilde{\alpha}_{\rm spec}^{\rm BOE}=1$, between the permutation-symmetric and the specialized phases. In contrast, for $\beta\lambda<1$, the generalization error in the PS phase exceeds more than twice the Bayes-optimal error and increases as $\lambda$ decreases, while the transition point remains at $\Tilde{\alpha}_{\rm spec}^{\rm BO}$ and the generalization error in the specialized phase saturates at twice the optimal one. For $\beta\lambda>1$, the specialization transitions shifts to $\Tilde{\alpha}_{\rm spec} > \Tilde{\alpha}_{\rm spec}^{\rm BOE}$, with $\Tilde{\alpha}_{\rm spec}(\beta\lambda > 1) \to \infty$ as $\lambda \to \infty$, preventing the student from learning. In this $\beta\lambda>1$ regime, the Gibbs generalization error approaches the optimal one within the permutation-symmetric phase.  These observations indicate that the \emph{optimal value of weight decay $\lambda$ is finite and phase-dependent}. Similar phenomena have been observed in convex one-layer models, where either optimally tuning the regularization or maximizing the flatness of the solution can lead to Bayes-optimal generalization~\cite{Aubin2020,Mignacco2020,Baldassi_2020}. Additionally, in this noisy setting, a memorization branch exists, and depending on the noise covariance, it can correspond to a metastable state -- especially for sufficiently large  $\Delta^\star$ where the teacher label are mostly noise. 

In the right panel of Fig.~\ref{fig:ERM_vs_BOE}, we analyze the noiseless case ($\Delta^\star=0$). For finite weigh decay $\lambda>0$, ERM exhibits a specialization transition at $\Tilde{\alpha}_{\rm spec}$, separating memorization and specialization phases. Beyond the transition, the memorization branch persists as a metastable state. As $\lambda$ decreases, the ERM generalization error approaches the BOE, which vanishes beyond the transition. Notably, the memorization phase is absent in the Bayes-optimal case, highlighting a qualitative difference between ERM and the Bayesian learning. Based on our discussion of the noisy setting, this behavior suggests that as $\lambda\to0$, the memorization branch exchanges stability with the PS one, and $\Tilde{\alpha}_{\rm spec}$ marks the boundary between the PS and specialized phase.


