\section{Discussion and conclusion}
In this paper, we developed a theory for the typical generalization performance of a narrow one-hidden layer network in the presence of random i.i.d. data in the teacher-student scenario, for small temperature Bayesian learning and empirical risk minimization. Our theory highlights the presence of different phases and thermodynamic states, some corresponding to metastable ones able to trap the dynamics of full-batch GD both in the presence and absence of explicit noise. We further showed how the activation function is responsible for the appearance of different kinds of transitions in a Bayesian setting, in a manner that depends on the value of the temperature parameter. Interestingly, we identified a memorization phase in which the student network breaks permutation symmetry among its hidden units without aligning to the teacher's weights.
%Interestingly, we identified the presence of a memorization phase, where the permutation symmetry among hidden units in the student network is broken, while at the same time showing no alignment on the teacher weight vectors.

Our work paves the way for additional investigations of more complex network models and input distributions. One limitation of this work is the focus on i.i.d. inputs, which considerably simplifies analytical derivations. Addressing the problem of learning in the presence of structured inputs is crucial to model the learning performance on real datasets and is left for future work. For instance, we expect our analytical framework to be generalizable to input distributions expressed as mixtures of Gaussian with generic mean vectors and covariance matrices.

Learning in two-layer networks in the $K\ll N$ regime has been recently analyzed using dynamical mean field theory for gradient flow~\cite{montanari_dfmt_2025}. The authors consider both first- and second-layer learnable weights and identify different dynamical regimes using a separation of time-scales argument. It would be interesting to characterize the steady states of such learning dynamics, in relation to the equilibrium distribution induced by Langevin-based sampling of the weight posterior. In doing so, one should generalize our framework to the case of plastic second-layer weights, which we considered quenched for simplicity. This would also help in fully clarifying the difference between the $K\ll N$ case and the so-called proportional regime, i.e. with $N=O\left(K\right)$ and $P=O\left( N \right)$, see \cite{pacelli2023statistical} and references therein.

