\title{Efficient space reduction techniques by optimized majority rules for the Kemeny aggregation problem}


\author[A]{\fnms{Xuan Kien}~\snm{Phung}}

\author[A]{\fnms{Sylvie}~\snm{Hamel}}


\address[A]{Département d'informatique et de recherche opérationnelle, Université de Montréal, Canada}



\begin{abstract}
The Kemeny aggregation problem consists of computing the consensus rankings of an election with respect to the Kemeny-Young voting method. These aggregated rankings are the geometric medians as well as the maximum likelihood estimators in the Mallows model of the rankings in the election under the Kendall-tau distance which counts the number of pairwise disagreements. The problem admits fundamental applications in various domains such as computational social choice, machine learning, operations research, and biology but its computational complexity is unfortunately expensive. In this paper, we establish optimized quantitative extensions of the well-known 3/4-majority rule of Betzler et al. and the Major Order Theorem of Hamel and Milosz for the Kemeny aggregation problem. By taking into account the extra information available in the problem such as the number of candidates and by considering an additional optimization of certain piecewise linear functions in one variable, our results achieve significantly more refined space reduction techniques as illustrated by experimental results on real and synthetic data without increasing the time complexity of the algorithms. 
\end{abstract}

\end{frontmatter}


\section{Introduction} 
Rank aggregation is one of the most intensive studied problems with a wide range of high-stake applications in many domains such as biology, machine learning, computational choice, and  operations research. Following the terminologies in social choice theory, an election $(C,V)$ is  a finite collection $C=\{c_1, \dots, c_n\}$ of candidates (alternatives) together with a voting profile $V$ consisting of a finite number of votes not necessarily distinct. Here, a ranking or a vote is simply a complete and strict total ordering $\pi \colon c_{\pi(1)}> c_{\pi(2)}> \dots >c_{\pi(n)}$ which we identify with a permutation of elements of $C$. The notation $x>y$ means that $x$ is ranked before $y$. Among several natural distances of the space of all rankings, the Kendall-tau distance, which is also the bubble-sort distance between two permutations, is one of the most prominent distances that counts the number of order disagreements between pairs of elements in two permutations. 
\par 
In the well-known Kemeny problem \cite{kemeny}, \cite{kemeny-snell}, \cite{young}, the objective is to determine the set of medians which are permutations whose total Kendall-tau distance to the voting profile is minimized. Hence,  a median is simply a ranking 
that maximizes the number of pairwise agreements with the voting profile. Among many desirable properties satified by the Kemeny voting rule from the social choice theory point of view, a median is a maximum likelihood estimator of the correct ranking  \cite{kemeny} in the statisical Mallows model \cite{mallows}. 
\par 

Computing the medians of an election is unfortunately NP-hard \cite{dwork}, \cite{bachmeier} thus known exact algorithms are computationally expensive and impractical for large-scale instances. Most of the  literature thus concentrate on the approximation  algorithms and heuristics \cite{Kemenywww}. A reasonable and intermediate approach is to obtain exact algorithm and techniques to reduce the search space, e.g. by reducing to subproblems of smaller size with the Extended Condorcet Criteria (XCC) \cite{truchon-XCC}, the 3/4 Majority rule \cite{3/4} or by simplifying the weighted majority graph of the election with the MOT algorithm \cite{hamel-space}, the Unanimity property or the Pareto efficiency \cite{always}.        
\par 
Our main contribution consists of optimized quantitative extensions
of the well-known 3/4-majority rule of Betzler et al. \cite{3/4} and the Major
Order Theorem (MOT) of Hamel and Milosz \cite{hamel-space} for the Kemeny aggregation
problem. These rules are majority rules in the sense that they both provide sufficient conditions to confirm that the relative rankings of two candidates $x,y$ in all medians must respect the preference of the majority of the votes.
\par 
Our extensions improve significantly the efficiency of the above techniques and have the potential to deal with a good proportion of  large-scale instances with hundreds of candidates by using a combination of these optimized majority rules and any black box solver that can solve exactly small-medium instances up to 30-50 candidates.  
\par 
By taking into account the extra information available in
the problem such as the number of candidates and by considering an
additional optimization of certain piecewise linear functions in one
variable, our results achieve significantly more refined space reduction techniques as illustrated by experimental results on real-world data from PrefLib and synthetic data drawn randomly from the Mallows model for   ranking distribution. 


\section{Notations and definitions}

\subsection{Kemeny voting rule}
Let $C$ be a finite set of candidates. Let $S(C)$ be the set of all rankings of $C$. Let $\Delta(C)=\{\{x,y\}\colon x, y \in C, x\neq y\}$ be the collection of all distinct pairs of elements of $C$. The \emph{Kendall-tau distance} $ d_{KT}(\pi, \sigma)$ between two rankings $\pi, \sigma$ of $C$ is defined by: 
\begin{equation}
\label{e:definition-k-wise-distance}
    d_{KT}(\pi, \sigma) = \sum_{\{x,y\}\in \Delta(C)} \textbf{1}_{\mathrm{top}_{\{x,y\}(\pi)} \neq  \mathrm{top}_{\{x,y\}(\sigma)}} 
\end{equation}
where $\mathrm{top}_{\{x,y\}(\pi)} \in \{x,y\}$ denotes the highest ranked element in the induced ranking $\pi\vert_{\{x,y\}}$ of $\pi$ on $\{x,y\}$. 
The Kendall-tau distance between a ranking $\pi$ of $C$ and a collection of rankings $A$ of $C$ is defined formally as $
    d_{KT}(\pi,A) = \sum_{\sigma \in A}  d_{KT}(\pi,\sigma)$. 
\par 
Let $V$ be the voting profile of the election. Then we say that a ranking $\pi$ of $C$ is a \emph{median of the election} or simply a \emph{median} if 
\begin{equation*}
    d_{KT}(\pi,V) = \min_{\sigma \in S(C)} d_{KT} (\sigma, V). 
\end{equation*}
From the voting profile $V$, we can construct the associated  weighted majority graph where the vertices are the candidates and an arc from a vertex $x$ to another vertex $y$ is weighted by the proportion $a_{xy}$ of the votes in which $x$ is ranked before $y$. Note that $a_{xy}+a_{yx}=1$. The weighted majority graph can thus be presented by the matrix $[\delta_{xy}]_{x,y\in C}\in \R^{n \times n}$ where $a_{xy}=0$ if $x=y$ and otherwise,  
$$\delta_{xy}= a_{xy} - a_{yx}.$$


\subsection{The 3/4-majority rule}
 Given $s \in [1/2,1]$, we say that a candidate $x$ is $s$-non-dirty for the election $(C,V)$ if for every other candidate $y$, we have $x\geq_{s}y$ or $y\geq_s x$. The following fundamental result is proved in  \cite{3/4}.  
\begin{theorem}[3/4-majority rule] 
If $x$ is a $3/4$-non-dirty candidate for $(C,V)$ then in every median, $x$ is ranked before $y$ whenever $x\geq_{3/4}y$ and $y$ is ranked before $x$ whenever $y\geq_{3/4}x$.  
\end{theorem}
\par 
\vspace{4pt}
Hence, if such a 3/4-non-dirty candidate $x$ exists, every median has the form $L(x)^*>x>R(x)^*$
where $L(x)= \{y \in C\setminus\{x\} : y \geq_{3/4} x\}$, $R(x)= \{y \in C\setminus\{x\} : x \geq_{3/4} y\}$, and $L(x)^*$, resp. $R(x)^*$, are the medians  of the induced election $(L(x), V\vert_{L(x)})$ obtained by removing in every vote the candidates not in $L(x)$, resp. of the induced election $(R(x), V\vert_{R(x)})$ obtained by removing in every vote the candidates not in $R(x)$. In other words, the 3/4-majority rule allows us to divide the original problem into small subproblems and it suffices to concatenate the medians of the subproblems and the 3/4-non-dirty candidate to obtain medians of the problem. 
\par 
We can obviously applying the 3/4-majority rule to each induced election $(L(x), V\vert_{L(x)})$ and $(R(x), V\vert_{R(x)})$. By repeating the above procedure, we obtain the iterated version of 3/4-majority rule whose running time is in  $O(n^2m)$.  
Let $d_a(V)$ be the average Kendall-tau distance between 2 votes in the voting profile $V$. It was shown in \cite{3/4} that the number of 3/4-non-dirty candidates is at least $n-16d_a(V)/3$. Conversely, experimental results also show that the 3/4-majority rule works best when the votes are close to each other. 



 \subsection{The Major Order Theorem (MOT)} 
\label{s:mot}
Various versions of  MOT in \cite{hamel-space} provide some of the most efficient preprocessing techniques to reduce the search space of the Kemeny problem. MOT aims to determine pairs $\{x,y\}$ of candidates such that $x$ is ranked before $y$ in every median whenever $\delta_{xy}\geq 0$. Thus we can simplify the weighted majority graph of the election by setting $\delta_{xy}=1$ if $\delta_{xy} \geq 0$ for all pairs $\{x,y\}$ solved by MOT. 
\par 
More specifically, we define the inference set   $E_{xy}$ as the collection (with possible repetitions of elements) of candidates  $z$ such that $x>z>y$ in some vote. The  multiplicity of $z$ in $E_{xy}$ is the number of  votes in which  $x>z>y$. 

\begin{theorem}[{MOT}]  
    \label{2-wise-major-order}  
If $x>_{1/2}y$ and $\delta_{xy} > |E_{yx} \setminus E_{xy}|$ then $x$ is ranked before   $y$ in every median.    
 \end{theorem}
\par 
MOT's running time is $O(n^3)$. 
% La preuve du MOT est basé sur une estimation directe de la différence $2d_{KT}(\pi, V)$ et $d_{KT}(\pi_1, V)+d_{KT}(\pi_2, V)$ où $\pi=...yKx...$, $\pi_1=...xyK...$ et $\pi_2= ...Kxy...$.  
 If we iterate MOT by removing from $E_{xy}$ all the candidates which are ranked between $x$ et $y$ by the $i$ previous iterations, we obtain $(i+1)$-th inference set  $E^{(i+1)}_{xy} \subseteq E^{(i)}_{xy} $. This gives us the iterated version of MOT. 

\begin{theorem}[{Iterated MOT}]    
    If $x>_{1/2}y$ and if  $\delta_{xy} > |E^{(i)}_{yx} \setminus E^{(i)}_{xy}|$ for some $i \geq 0$, then $x$ is ranked before $y$ in every median.   
 \end{theorem}
\par 


\section{The $(\alpha, \beta)$-majority rule}

We first explain our $(\alpha, \beta)$-majority rule which use linear combination with variable coefficients to improve the 3/4-majority rule. A similar idea will also be applied to improve various versions of MOT. We say that a candidate $x$ is $(\alpha, \beta)$-non-dirty if for every other candidate $y$ either $x\geq_{\beta} y$ or $y\geq_\alpha x$. 
\begin{theorem}[$(\alpha, \beta)$-majority rule]
\label{t:alpha-beta-rule} Let $\alpha, \beta \in [1/2,1]$ such that $\alpha+\beta \geq 3/2$. Suppose that a candidate $x$ is $(\alpha, \beta)$-non-dirty. Then in every   median,  $x$ is ranked before $y$ whenever $x\geq_{\beta}y$ and $y$ is ranked before $x$ whenever $y\geq_{\alpha}x$.  
\end{theorem}

Note that the case $\alpha=\beta=3/4$ corresponds to the 3/4-majority rule of Betzler et al. 



\begin{proof} 
Let $C$ be the set of all candidates in the election and let $V$ be the set of all votes.   
Suppose on the contrary that there exists a 2-wise median $r$ together with a candidate $y$ and an $(\alpha, \beta)$-non-dirty candidate $x$ such that $x >_{1/2} y$ but $y>^rx$. The case $y >_{1/2} x$ but $x>^ry$ is similar by symmetry. 
\par 
Let $K$ be the ordered set of all candidates ranked between $x$ and $y$ in $r$. It follows that $y >^r K >^rx$. Since $x$ is $(\alpha, \beta)$-non-dirty, we have a partition $K= L \coprod R$ where  
\begin{equation*}
    L = \{z \in K \colon z \geq_\alpha x\}, \quad R = \{z \in K \colon z \leq_\beta x\}.  
\end{equation*}
\par 
Let $m=|V|$, $k=|K|$, and $a=|R|$ so that $|L|=k-a$. 
Consider the modified ranking $r^*$ in which $L > x > y >R$. We will show $r^*$ has lower Kemeny score than $r$. Indeed, the only pairs of candidates which can contribute to the score difference $\Delta = d_{KT}(r^*, V) - d_{KT}(r, V)$ are $(x,y)$, $(x, R)$, $(y, L)$, and $(L,R)$ where e.g. $(x,R)$ means any pair $(x,z)$ where $z \in R$ and similarly for $(y, L)$ and $(L,R)$. 
\par 
Since $x \geq_\beta y$, the pair $(x,y)$ contributes at most $m - \beta  m$ to $d_{KT}(r^*, V)$ but contributes at least $ \beta m$ to $d_{KT}(r, V)$. Hence, the contribution of $(x,y)$ to $\Delta$ is at most
\begin{align}
\label{e:alpha-beta-1}
m - \beta m-  \beta m = (1-2\beta )m.
\end{align}
Note that if $\beta=1/2$ then since $x>_{1/2}y$, the  the contribution of $(x,y)$ to $\Delta$ is at strictly less than 
\begin{align}
\label{e:alpha-beta-1/2}
m - \beta m-  \beta m = (1-2\beta )m.
\end{align}
\noindent
Similarly, we find that the contribution of all pairs $(x,R)$ to the difference $\Delta$ is at most 
\begin{align}
\label{e:alpha-beta-2}
(1-2\beta)ma
\end{align}
\par 
For every $z \in L$ and $t \in R$, we have $z\geq_\alpha x$ and $x\geq_\beta t$. Therefore, $z > x >t$ and thus $z>t$ in at least $(\alpha+\beta -1)m$ votes. It follows that the  pairs $(R,L)$ contribute at most 
\begin{align}
\label{e:alpha-beta-3}
(1- 2(\alpha +\beta -1))ma(k-a) = (3-2(\alpha +\beta))ma(k-a). 
\end{align}
\noindent
Similarly, for every $z \in L$, we find that $z > x >y$ and thus $z>y$ in at least 
$(\alpha +\beta -1)m$ votes. Consequently, the contribution of the  pairs $(L,y)$ to the score difference $\Delta$ at most 
\begin{align}
\label{e:alpha-beta-4}
(1-2(\alpha +\beta-1))m(k-a)= (3-2(\alpha +\beta))m(k-a) 
\end{align}
\par 
By combining the inequalities \eqref{e:alpha-beta-1}, \eqref{e:alpha-beta-2}, \eqref{e:alpha-beta-3}, and \eqref{e:alpha-beta-4}, we see that $\Delta$ can be bounded as follows:  
\begin{align}
\label{e:alpha-beta-betzler}
    \frac{\Delta}{m}  \leq & (1-2\beta) + (1-2\beta)a + (3-2(\alpha +\beta))a(k-a) \nonumber  \\
    & + (3-2(\alpha +\beta))(k-a) \\
     =  & (a+1)(1-2\beta +(3-2(\alpha +\beta))(k-a)) \nonumber\\
 \leq & (a+1)(1-2\beta )  \nonumber
\end{align}
where the second inequality follows from the fact that $\alpha+\beta \geq 3/2$ and $k-a \geq 0$. 
 If $\beta>1/2$ then the above estimation shows that  $\Delta/m \leq (a+1)(1-2\beta ) <0$ since $a\geq 0$. 
If $\beta=1/2$ then by the remark \eqref{e:alpha-beta-1/2}, the inequality \eqref{e:alpha-beta-betzler} is strict and thus $\Delta/m <(a+1)(1-2\beta )=0$. 
In any case, we have $\Delta<0$ which contradicts the choice of $r^*$. The proof is thus complete.  
\end{proof}  
\noindent 
\textbf{Remark.} In the above proof, one can show that $K=LR$ as an ordered set so that the contribution of the pairs $(L,R)$ to $\Delta$ is zero. Indeed, it follows from the fact that the ordering of adjacent candidates in a median is simply determined by strict majority. 
\par 

To further obtain good $(\alpha, \beta)$-non-dirty candidate, we may take into account (1) the number $n$ of candidates and (2) the number of known candidates ranking before or after a potential non-dirty candidate. We first describe  a strengthened version of the $(\alpha, \beta)$-majority rule by using the number of candidates in the election. 

\begin{theorem} 
[Optimized $(\alpha, \beta)$-majority rule] 
\label{t:alpha-beta-rule-improved-1} 
Let $n \geq 3$ and let $\alpha, \beta \geq 1/2$ be such that $\alpha+\frac{n-1}{n-2}\beta > \frac{3}{2} + \frac{1}{2(n-2)}$. In an election with $n$ candidates, suppose that  $x$ is an $(\alpha, \beta)$-non-dirty candidate. Then in every Kemeny median of the election, the relative position of $x$ with every other candidate respects the strict majority rule.  
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
   Supposing $\beta > 1/2$, we proceed as in the proof of Theorem~\ref{t:alpha-beta-rule} to arrive at the following  estimation when $k-a>0$ (the case $\beta=1/2$ or $k-a=0$ is immediate): 
\begin{align}
\label{e:alpha-beta-betzler-improved-1}
    \frac{\Delta}{m}  \leq & (1-2\beta) + (1-2\beta)a + (3-2(\alpha +\beta))a(k-a) \nonumber  \\
    & + (3-2(\alpha +\beta))(k-a) \\
     =  & (a+1)(1-2\beta +(3-2(\alpha +\beta))(k-a)) \nonumber\\
 = & 
  2(a+1)(k-a)\left(\frac{1-2\beta}{2(k-a)} +\frac{3}{2} - \alpha - \beta\right) \nonumber\\
  \leq &  2(a+1)(k-a)\left(\frac{1-2\beta}{2(n-2)} +\frac{3}{2} - \alpha - \beta\right)
 \nonumber\\
 \leq &  2(a+1)(k-a)\left(\frac{3}{2} + \frac{1}{2(n-2)} - \alpha- \frac{n-1}{n-2}\beta \right)< 0 \nonumber 
\end{align}
and the conclusion follows. 
\end{proof}


  %   Let $a= 2m(\alpha-0.5)$ and $b=2m(\beta -0.5)$. The condition $\alpha+\frac{n-1}{n-2}\beta > \frac{3}{2} + \frac{1}{2(n-2)}$ can be rewritten neatly as 
  %   $$
  % a+\frac{n-1}{n-2}b >m.
  %   $$


We now describe how to improve the above result by considering available information on the positions of the candidate. 

\begin{theorem}
\label{t:alpha-beta-rule-improved-2} Let $p  \geq 1$ and let $\alpha, \beta \geq 1/2$ be such that $\alpha+\frac{p+1}{p}\beta > \frac{3}{2} + \frac{1}{2p}$. Let $x$ be an $(\alpha, \beta)$-non-dirty candidate such that $x$ is ranked among the first $p+1$ candidates, resp. among the last  $p+1$ candidates, in every median. If $x>_{1/2}y$, resp. if  $y>_{1/2}x$, then $x> y$, resp.  $y> x$, in every median.  
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
   Suppose that $x$ is ranked among the first $p+1$ candidates (the other case is similar). The proof is then similar to that of Theorem~\ref{t:alpha-beta-rule-improved-1}. However,   we only assume $x$ is ranked among the first $p+1$ candidates in every median but do not assume that $x$ is ranked among the last $p+1$ candidates in every median. Hence, we cannot use symmetry to conclude that   $x< y$ in every median whenever  $x<_{1/2}y$.    
\end{proof}


Consequently, suppose that for some $p\geq n/2$, we have an $(\alpha, \beta)$-non-dirty candidate $x$ such that $x$ is ranked among the first $p+1$ candidates \emph{and} also among the last $p+1$ candidates in every median (such information on the position of $x$  might be achieved from known non-dirty candidates or using  $(\alpha, \beta)$-MOT).   
The conclusion  of Theorem~\ref{t:alpha-beta-rule-improved-2} 
 then allows us to divide the set of candidates different from $x$ into two subsets $L$, $R$ such that $z> x>  y$ in every median for every $z \in L$ and $y \in R$. 

\par 

\subsection{Algorithm for Optimized $(\alpha,\beta)$-majority rule}\label{s:algo-alpha-beta}


\RestyleAlgo{ruled}

\SetKwComment{Comment}{//* }{ *//}



\begin{algorithm}[hbt!]
\caption{Optimized $(\alpha,\beta)$-Majority Rule}\label{alg:abMaj}
\DontPrintSemicolon
\SetKw{KwRet}{return}
\SetAlgoLined
\SetKwProg{Fn}{Function}{:}{end}
\SetKwFunction{KwFn}{abMaj} 
\KwData{$C$, matrix $[\delta_{xy}]\in \R^{n \times n}$,  threshold $t \geq 2$} 
\KwResult{ouput the number of candidates with known positions in the medians of the election}

\Fn{\KwFn{$A$}}{
\If{$|A|\leq t$}{\KwRet $|A|$}
\For{$x \in A$}{
$L=\{z\in A\setminus\{x\}\colon \delta_{xz} \geq 0\}$\; $R=\{z\in A\setminus\{x\} \colon \delta_{zx} > 0\}$\;
$\alpha=\displaystyle   \left(1+\min \left\{\ \delta_{zx} \colon z\in A, \, \delta_{zx}\geq 0 \right\}\right)/2$\; $\beta=\displaystyle \left( 1 + \min \left\{\ \delta_{xz} \colon z\in A, \, \delta_{xz}\geq 0 \right\}\right)/2$\;
% $X \gets x$\;
% $N \gets n$\;
\If{$  \alpha+\frac{|A|-1}{|A|-2}\beta > \frac{3}{2} + \frac{1}{2(|A|-2)}$}{\KwRet \KwFn{$L$} $+$ \KwFn{$R$} $+1$\,\,}
}
\KwRet 0\; 
}
\KwFn{$C$} \;  

\end{algorithm}


Once an $(\alpha, \beta)$-non-dirty candidate $x$ is identified with $\alpha, \beta \in (1/2,1]$, the relative position of $x$ and any other candidate $y$ is completely solved by the majority rule. {In other words, the exact position of $x$ is known and is the same in every median}. Then, it suffices to find the medians on each of the subsets of candidates 
\begin{equation*}
    L= \{z  \colon z \geq_\alpha x\}, \quad R = \{z \colon x >_\beta z\} 
\end{equation*}
and concatenate the results to obtain the medians of the original election. 
Consequently, we deduce the following {practical iterated algorithm}  which extends the reduction rule of Betzler et al. 
\par 
We begin with the set  of all candidates. At each step, we deal with the induced election on a certain subset $A$ of candidates. We compute all good $(\alpha, \beta)$-non-dirty candidates (if they exist) $x_1,..., x_k  $ of $A$ as well as the corresponding parameters $(\alpha_1, \beta_1)$, ...,  $(\alpha_k, \beta_k)$. To find such candidates, we compute for each  $x \in A$ the sets 
\begin{equation*}
    L(x,A)= \{z \in A \colon \delta_{xz}\geq 0\}, \quad R(x,A) = \{z\in A \colon \delta_{zx}>0 \} 
\end{equation*}
and test for
\begin{align*}
\alpha(x,A) &=\min \{s  : z\geq_{s} x, \, \delta_{zx}\geq 0\}\\
\beta(x,A) &=\min \{s  : x\geq_{s} z, \,\delta_{xz} \geq 0  \}
\end{align*}
whether $\alpha, \beta \geq 1/2$ and $\alpha+\frac{n-1}{n-2}\beta > \frac{3}{2} + \frac{1}{2(n-2)}$. We call such a candidate $x$  good $(\alpha, \beta)$-non-dirty candidate of $A$. 
\par 
The exact positions of these candidates $x_1,..., x_k$ are  determined in every median of the induced election over $A$ and of the original election. We can also completely order 
$x_1, ..., x_k$ thus without loss of generality suppose that $x_1>x_2>...>x_k$. Let $A_1$, respectively $A_{k+1}$, be the sets of candidates in $A$ which rank before $x_1$, respectively after $x_k$. For $i\in \{2,..., k\}$, let $A_i$ be the set of candidates in $A$ which rank between $x_{i-1}$ and $x_{i}$. 
\par 
Then we repeat the procedure for each of the induced elections over $A_1$,..., $A_{k+1}$. A candidate $x\in A$ may fail to be a good $(\alpha, \beta)$-non-dirty candidate for the induced election over $A$ but $x$ may very well be a good $(\alpha, \beta)$-non-dirty candidate for the induced election over some subset $A_i$. This feature explains the interest of the iterative nature of the algorithm. 
Finally, the algorithm returns a partial ranking of the form 
$$
T_1z_1T_2z_2T_3....T_lz_lT_{l+1}
$$
where $z_1,...,z_l$ are candidates and the sets $T_1,..., T_{l+1}$ form a  partition of the remaining  candidates such that the induced election on each $T_i$ contains no good $(\alpha, \beta)$-non-dirty candidates. 
Note that by Theorem \ref{t:alpha-beta-rule}, every median of the original election must be of the above form. 
\par 
Using an exact solver for small-medium instances with up to $t$ candidates for some fixed threshold $t\geq 2$, we can directly solve the induced election on $T_i$ if $|T_i|\leq t$ instead of keep  applying $(\alpha, \beta)$-majority rule again to the induced election on $T_i$. If we are only interested in the statistics of the number of candidates with known positions in the medians, we can thus simply add $|T_i|$ to the result if  $|T_i|\leq t$. We obtain the corresponding Algorithm~\ref{alg:abMaj} for Optimized $(\alpha, \beta)$-majority rule. 
\par 
To further improve the above algorithm, we can add a pre-processing step using Iterated $(\alpha,\beta)$-MOT (Theorem~\ref{t:alpha-beta-2MOT-3.0} below) for each subset $A$ found in the algorithm to obtain the exact relative rankings of pairs of candidate before computing the constants $\alpha(x,A), \beta(x,A)$ for each $x \in A$. This pre-processing step will result in a potential increase of $\alpha(x,A), \beta(x,A)$ and thus increase the chance for $x$ to be a good $(\alpha, \beta)$-non-dirty candidate for $A$.  
\par 
Note that in the last step, the  Iterated $(\alpha,\beta)$-MOT applied to each of the set $T_i$ gives us some extra information on the relative rankings of elements in each $T_i$. This is the best we can obtain using Iterated $(\alpha,\beta)$-MOT and $(\alpha, \beta)$-majority rule together. Note that these two rules do not imply each other but they are rather complementary. While Iterated $(\alpha, \beta)$-MOT is powerful, it is more computationally expensive and suffers from the general difficulty of obtaining complete rankings from the list of pairs with solved relative rankings.    
\par 





\section{The $(\alpha,\beta)$-MOT} 

By a similar idea as in the proof of the $(\alpha, \beta)$-majority rule, we can improve various versions of MOT. For the notation, let $U\subset C^2$ be a set of pairs of candidates. We denote by $\overline{U} \supset U$ the \emph{transitive closure} of $U$ so that   
    if $(x,y), (y,z) \in U$ then $(x,z) \in \overline{U}$. We define also 
    \begin{align*} 
    L_{x}(U) & =\{z \colon (z,x)  \in \overline{U} \}, 
 &   L_{x,y}(U) & =\{z \colon (z,x), (z,y) \in \overline{U} \},&\\
 R_{x}(U) & =\{z \colon (x,z) \in \overline{U} \}, & R_{x,y}(U) & =\{z \colon (x,z), (y,z) \in \overline{U} \}. & 
 \end{align*} 
\begin{theorem}
\label{t:alpha-beta-2MOT-3.0} 
Let $V$ be an election over a set of alternatives $C$. 
% Let $U_0$ be the set consisting of ordered pairs $(x,y)$ of distinct alternatives satisfying Theorem~\ref{3-wise-major-order}.
Let $W_{-1}=\varnothing$ and 
for every $k \geq 0$, we define $W_k$  inductively as the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)$ of distinct alternatives such that
\begin{align}
\label{e:alpha-beta-2-mot-condition}
     \delta_{xy}  > \min_{\alpha, \beta\geq 0,\,  \alpha+\beta=1} \sum_{z\in Z_k}  \max\left(0, \alpha \delta_{yz} + \beta \delta_{zx} \right)
\end{align}
where 
\begin{align} 
Z_k= C\setminus \left( L_{y}\left(\overline{W}_{k-1}\right) \cup R_{x}\left(\overline{W}_{k-1}\right) \cup \{x,y\}\right).
\end{align} 
Suppose that  $(x,y) \in \overline{W}_{k}$ for  some   $k \geq 0$. Then $x$ is ranked before $y$ in every median of the election. 
\end{theorem}


\begin{proof} 
We proceed by induction on $k \geq -1$. The case $k=-1$ is trivial since $W_{-1}=\varnothing$. Assume that the conclusion holds for some integer $k \geq -1$ and let $(x,y) \in W_{k+1}$. Suppose on the contrary that  $\pi \colon L>y>Z>x>R$ is a $2$-wise median of the election where $L,Z,R$ are ordered sets of alternatives. 
Let  $\sigma_1^* \colon L>Z>x>y>R$ and  $\sigma^*_2 \colon L>x>y>Z>R$.  Let $
 \Delta_i= d_{KT}^2(\pi, V) - d_{KT}^2(\sigma_i^*, V)$ where $i\in \{1,2\}$. Let $\alpha, \beta \geq 0$ such that $\alpha+\beta=1$ and $\sum_{z\in Z_k}  \max\left(0, \alpha \delta_{yz} + \beta \delta_{zx} \right)$ is minimized. We find by a direct  inspection that:  
 \begin{align}
  \label{e:alpha-beta-2-mot}
 \displaystyle 
    \alpha \Delta_1 +  \beta \Delta_2 & =  \left(\alpha  \delta_{xy} -  \alpha \sum_{z \in Z}  \delta_{yz} \right) + \left( \beta \delta_{xy} -  \beta \sum_{z \in Z}\delta_{zx} \right)\nonumber  \\
    & = (\alpha+\beta) \delta_{xy} -    \sum_{z \in Z} \left(\alpha \delta_{yz} + \beta \delta_{zx} \right)\nonumber \\
     &=   \delta_{xy} -    \sum_{z \in Z} \left(\alpha  \delta_{yz} + \beta \delta_{zx} \right) 
 \end{align} 
 since $\alpha+\beta =1$. 
By induction hypothesis, 
$L_{y}\left(\overline{W}_{k}\right) \subset L$ and $R_{x}\left(\overline{W}_k\right) \subset R$   thus  
\begin{align*}
Z \subset Z_k = C\setminus \left(L_{y}\left(\overline{W}_k\right) \cup R_{x}\left(\overline{W}_k\right) \cup \{x,y\} \right). 
\end{align*}
\par 
\noindent
Since $(x,y) \in W_{k+1}$ and $Z\subset Z_k$, we deduce from   \eqref{e:alpha-beta-2-mot-condition}, \eqref{e:alpha-beta-2-mot}, and the choice of $\alpha, \beta$ that 
\begin{align*}
    \alpha \Delta_1 + \beta \Delta_2&=   \delta_{xy} -    \sum_{z \in Z} \left(\alpha  \delta_{yz} + \beta \delta_{zx} \right) \\
& \geq \delta_{xy} -    \sum_{z \in Z_k} \max \left(0, \alpha \delta_{yz} + \beta \delta_{zx} \right) >0.  
\end{align*}
\par 
\noindent
Hence, $\Delta_i >0$ for some $i \in \{1,2\}$ and $\sigma_i^*$ is a strictly better $2$-wise consensus than $\pi$, which contradicts the choice of $\pi$. Thus, $x$ is ranked before $y$ in every $2$-wise median whenever $(x,y) \in W_{k+1}$. By transitivity, $x$ must be ranked before $y$ in every $2$-wise median whenever $(x,y) \in \overline{W}_{k+1}$. 
The proof is complete. 
\end{proof} 
\noindent 
\textbf{Remark.} Since $Z\subset Z_k$ can be a priori any subset of candidates, the proof given above does not guarantee that the weaker  condition 
$$
\delta_{xy}  > \min \left( \sum_{z\in Z_k}   \delta_{yz}, \sum_{z\in Z_k} \delta_{zx}  \right)
$$
 is sufficient to conclude that $x$ is ranked before $y$ in every Kemeny median of the election. Ideally, we would like to verify the condition $ \max(\Delta_1, \Delta_2)>0$ or equivalently for \emph{all possible subsets} $Z\subset Z_k$, 
 $$
2\delta_{xy} -    \sum_{z \in Z} \left(  \delta_{yz} +  \delta_{zx} \right) >0  
 $$
but there is an exponential such subsets so the computation is not tractable. However, this condition can be precisely expressed as the condition \eqref{e:alpha-beta-2-mot-condition} which is now tractable (see Section~\ref{s:algo-iterated-ab-mot}).  



\subsection{Algorithm for Iterated $(\alpha, \beta)$-MOT} 
\label{s:algo-iterated-ab-mot}

Give Theorem~\ref{t:alpha-beta-2MOT-3.0}, the main task reduces to efficiently compute  the RHS of the condition  \eqref{e:alpha-beta-2-mot-condition}.  For this, let us rewrite the RHS of \eqref{e:alpha-beta-2-mot-condition} as a function $F_k\colon [0,1] \to \R$ of one variable $\alpha$: 
$$
F_k(\alpha)=   \sum_{z\in Z_k}  \max\left(0, \alpha \delta_{yz} + (1-\alpha) \delta_{zx} \right)=\sum_{z\in Z_k} f_{x,y,z}(\alpha). 
$$
where $f_{x,y,z}(\alpha)= \max\left(0, \alpha \delta_{yz} + (1-\alpha) \delta_{zx} \right)$ and $\alpha\in [0,1]$. 
First, observe  that $F_k$ is a continuous piecewise linear function  as  a sum of piecewise linear  functions $f_{x,y,z}$ on $[0,1]$. Second,  consider the critical points $0=t_1, \dots, t_r=1$ of $F_k$ on the interval $[0,1]$ which are points of discontinuous of $F_k$ plus the two  boundary points $0$ and $1$ of the interval $[0,1]$. These points   form a subset of the union $T_k$ of critical points of the functions $f_{x,y,z}$ for all $z \in Z_k$. Note that 
\begin{itemize}
    \item if $\delta_{yz}\delta_{zx} \geq 0$, the critical points of $f_{x,y,z}$ are $0$ and $1$; 
    \item if  $\delta_{yz}\delta_{zx} < 0$, the critical points of $f_{x,y,z}$ are  $0$, $1$, and $$\displaystyle \frac{\delta_{zx}}{\delta_{zx}-\delta_{yz}}\in (0,1).$$
    \end{itemize} 
    Hence, $r \leq |T_k|\leq   |Z_k| +2 \leq |C|-2+2=|C|=n$. 
    Finally, we can efficiently compute  
\begin{align}
 \label{e:F-alpha-beta} 
  \min_{\alpha\in [0,1]}   F_k(\alpha)=\min_{1 \leq i\leq r} F_k(t_i)=\min_{t\in T_k} F_k(t). 
\end{align}


In Theorem~\ref{t:alpha-beta-2MOT-3.0},  it can be shown that $\overline{W}_k \subseteq \overline{W}_{k+1}$. This property justifies the  condition $(x,y)\notin W$ in the loop for and thus provides some acceleration for the following algorithm of Iterated $(\alpha, \beta)$-MOT. 
 \begin{algorithm}[hbt!]
\caption{Iterated  $(\alpha,\beta)$-MOT}\label{alg:Iterated-abMOT}
\DontPrintSemicolon 
\KwData{$C$, matrix $[\delta_{xy}]\in \R^{n \times n}$}
\KwResult{output the list of ordered pairs of candidates solved by Theorem~\ref{t:alpha-beta-2MOT-3.0}} 
$\textit{len} \gets -1$\;
$W \gets \text{empty list}$\;
\While{$\text{len} < |W|$}{
  $\textit{len} \gets |W|$\;
  {\For{$(x,y) \in C^2$ : $\delta_{xy}>0$, $(x,y)\notin W$}{
   $Z \gets C\setminus \left( L_{y}\left({W}\right) \cup R_{x}\left({W} \right) \cup \{x,y\}\right)$ \; 
 $rhs\gets\displaystyle \min_{\alpha, \beta\geq 0,\,  \alpha+\beta=1} \sum_{z\in Z}  \max\left(0, \alpha \delta_{yz} + \beta \delta_{zx} \right)$ \Comment*[r]{use \eqref{e:F-alpha-beta} with $Z_k=Z$} \; 

    \If{$\delta_{xy}>rhs$}{append  $(x,y)$ to $W$}\;
    }
    $W\gets \overline{W}$\; 
  }
}
\KwRet{$W$} 
\end{algorithm}




\section{Majority rules combined}


Let $L$ be the set of all ordered pairs of candidates solved by (Iterated) $(\alpha, \beta)$-MOT. Let $G^{\alpha, \beta}(V)$ be the updated weighted majority graph in which the weight of an arc $(x,y)$ is updated to $1$ if $(x,y) \in L$. We then apply  $(\alpha, \beta)$-majority rule to this updated weighted majority graph $G^{\alpha, \beta}(V)$. The results obtained by $(\alpha, \beta)$-majority rule on the candidates with solved positions in the medians can in turn be used to update the list $L$. We can repeat the procedure all over again to obtain the iterated combination $(\alpha, \beta)$-MOT and $(\alpha, \beta)$-majority rule.  
\par 
In fact, the following result holds for general weighted tournament since (Iterated) $(\alpha, \beta)$-MOT and (optimized) $(\alpha, \beta)$-majority rule can be applied without modification to such settings. 


\begin{lemma}
\label{l:reduce-majority-graph}
    A ranking $\pi$ is a median for the original weighted majority graph $G(V)$ associated with the voting profile $V$ if and only if it is a median for the updated weighted majority graph $G^{\alpha, \beta}(V)$. 
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof} It suffices to note that by Theorem~\ref{t:alpha-beta-2MOT-3.0} every median for $G(V)$ agrees with every updated arc $(x,y)\in L$ and by the Unanimity property \cite{always}, every median for $G^{\alpha, \beta}(V)$ must also agree with the updated arcs in $L$ since they  all have weight 1 in $G^{\alpha, \beta}(V)$. The conclusion then follows from the definition of the Kendall-tau distance and the fact that all the corresponding arcs in $G(V)$ and $G^{\alpha, \beta}(V)$ except for those in $L$ have the same weights. 
% \par 
% In other words, the "if" part in the conclusion of the lemma is simply a reformulation of  Theorem~\ref{t:alpha-beta-2MOT-3.0}.   
\end{proof}

As a consequence, we can apply the $(\alpha, \beta)$-majority rule to the updated weighted majority graph $G^{\alpha, \beta}(V)$ to obtain more candidates with known  exact positions in the medians. 
\par 
As illustrated by the experimental results in Section~\ref{s:simulation}, running Iterated  $(\alpha, \beta)$-majority rule and  Iterated $(\alpha, \beta)$-MOT one after another increases drastically the number of pairs solved. It is not surprising since the conditions of   $(\alpha, \beta)$-majority rule and   $(\alpha, \beta)$-MOT are in a sense complementary. Hence, we can include the Iterated  $(\alpha, \beta)$-majority rule inside the Iterated $(\alpha, \beta)$-MOT to maximize the strength of these two algorithm. 
Because of a lower running time complexity,  Iterated  $(\alpha, \beta)$-majority does not increase the complexity of Iterated $(\alpha, \beta)$-MOT if they are run one after another. 





\section{Experimental results}
\label{s:simulation}

To illustrate the efficiency of our majority rules, we perform several simulations of our algorithms and their combinations  on real-world data from the library of preference data PrefLib as well as synthetic random data according to the Mallows distribution. 
\par 
For every incomplete voting profile which is marked with the extension .soi in PrefLib (complete voting profiles have the extension .soc), we put all the missing candidates of every incomplete vote in the last position in order to compute the matrix $[\delta_{xy}]$ of the voting profile and the weighted majority graph of the election. 
\par 
The Mallows distribution \cite{mallows} is given by 
$$\mathrm{P}(\pi)= \theta^{d_{KT}(\pi, \pi_0)}/Z$$ 
where $\pi_0$ denotes a fixed  centered vote, $\theta\in \, ]0,1]$ is the dispersion parameter, and $Z$ is the normalization constant. If $p\in [1/2,1]$ represents the probability that a voter can independently choose the better candidate (according to $\pi_0$) in each pair of  candidates, then $p=\displaystyle \frac{1}{1+\theta}$. In particular, when the value $\theta$ is close to $0$, the value of $p$ is b  close to $1/2$ and the votes in the voting profile are concentrated near $\pi_0$. On the other hand, when $\theta$ approaches $1$, the votes tend to be uniformly randomly distributed and the value of $p$ approaches $1/2$. 
\par 
In this section, the parameter $t$ represents the threshold used in Algorithm~\ref{alg:abMaj}  for the $(\alpha, \beta)$-majority rule. The overall experimental results show that our majority rules  $(\alpha, \beta)$-majority rule and  $(\alpha, \beta)$-MOT greatly outperform their original majority rules namely 3/4-majority rule MOT. $n$ respresents the number of candidates and $m$ respresents the number of votes in the election. 

\subsection{$(\alpha, \beta)$-majority rule vs 3/4-majority rule} 


Figure~\ref{fig:ab-vs-34} provides the number of candidates with exact known positions in the medians  solved by the 3/4-majority rule and the $(\alpha, \beta)$-majority rule   over some real-world data taken from the library PrefLib. 
Figures~\ref{fig:ab-maj-1}, \ref{fig:ab-maj-2}, \ref{fig:ab-maj-3}, \ref{fig:ab-maj-4} provide pairs of average numbers of candidates with exact known positions in the medians solved respectively by the 3/4-majority rule and the $(\alpha, \beta)$-majority rule over  100 instances for each line in the tables drawn form the Mallows distribution  
