\section{Introduction}


Living cells respond to external stimuli through mechanisms such as gene regulatory networks. Systems biology hopes to construct complex circuits such as oscillators/switches from simple building blocks. Recently, these designs have enabled the development of complicated biological circuits for real-world applications.
Our work aims to enable computation in living environments, where modern computers are currently unable to operate.

Reaction networks offer a robust framework for biomolecular interactions and can be realized through DNA strand displacement reactions, providing a physical basis in cellular environments. This makes them a powerful ``programming language" for molecular computation, owing to DNA’s sequence specificity and high-density information storage. However, programming reaction networks for more sophisticated tasks like training and testing remains a challenge.

The idea of using reaction networks for performing computation dates back to the 1940s. McCulloch and Pitt were the first to link neural networks to cellular automata~\cite{mcculloch1943logical}. In the 1960s, Jacob, Sugita, and Rossler used chemistry for implementing logical circuits~\cite{jacob1961genetic,rossler1974chemical,sugita1963functional}. Later, in 1994, Adleman solved a seven-node Hamiltonian problem using DNA strands~\cite{adleman1994molecular}. Subsequently, Winfree and Qian~\cite{cherry2018scaling,qian2011neural} have developed DNA strand displacement schemes to create more complex circuits. Recently, reaction networks have been used to implement various machine learning algorithms. In particular, these include the work of Stojanovic et al. \cite{pei2010training} who construct reaction networks to automate decision tree implementations and Gopalkrishnan et al. \cite{gopalkrishnan2016scheme,virinchi2017stochastic, virinchi2018reaction} who have designed reaction network schemes that calculate maximum likelihood estimates and simulate Boltzmann machines~\cite{poole2017chemical, poole2025autonomous, poole2022detailed,singh2019reaction}. More recently, reaction networks have been shown to implement neural network dynamics~\cite{anderson2021reaction,fan2023automatic, kang2024noise}.

In this paper, our focus is on Support Vector Machines (SVMs). Introduced by Vapnik and Chervonenkis in the 1960s, SVMs are supervised classification algorithms. The essential idea of SVM is to construct a hyperplane that maximally separates data points from different classes, creating the largest possible margin. For data that can be separated linearly, SVM works perfectly well, but for nonlinear cases, one needs to use the \emph{kernel functions} for mapping data onto spaces that are high-dimensional. 

In this work, we design a reaction network scheme to implement a Hard-Margin SVM, leveraging biochemical processes for machine learning tasks. Our reaction network-based model can load input data points, perform inference, back-propagate loss, and update weights via gradient descent. To ensure these operations proceed in a controlled and sequential manner, we utilize oscillating molecular species to achieve time-scale separation.
We validate our approach by simulating the reaction network on synthetic data in Python, demonstrating effective convergence with weight updates comparable to those of standard SVM library implementations. This work introduces a novel biochemical framework for executing complex machine learning algorithms beyond traditional computational systems, expanding the potential applications of reaction networks in computational and synthetic biology.

% In this paper, we design a reaction network scheme to implement a Hard-Margin Support Vector Machine (SVM). This reaction network-based model can load input data points, perform inference, back-propagate loss, and update weights through gradient descent. To perform these operations sequentially, we use oscillating molecules to achieve time-scale separation. We simulate the network on synthetic data in Python, demonstrating its ability to converge effectively, with weight updates comparable to those achieved by standard SVM library implementations. This approach provides a novel biochemical framework for executing complex machine learning algorithms in environments beyond traditional computational systems. 

\textbf{Structure of the paper: }
The paper is organized as follows. 
In Section~\ref{sec:reaction_networks}, we introduce reaction networks and recall their properties. Section~\ref{sec:support_vector_machines} introduces support vector machines and describes their functioning as an optimization problem. 
In Section~\ref{sec:modules}, we introduce the concept of dual-rail encoding to handle negative weights and biases. Further, we describe basic modules like addition, subtraction, multiplication, comparison and approximate majority that will be used as building blocks in the reaction network that simulates the SVM. 
Sections~\ref{sec:feedforward} and \ref{sec:learning} describe the reaction network that simulates the feedforward part and the learning part of SVM, respectively. In Section~\ref{sec:experimental_setup}, we introduce an experimental setup to train an SVM using a reaction network scheme. Our goal is to predict the weights and biases of the SVM obtained using reaction networks and compare them with the traditional machine learning implementation. In Section~\ref{sec:analysis}, we tabulate the values of weights and biases obtained using reaction networks and those obtained using the traditional SVM implementations. To validate these results, we also compare their trajectories as a function of time (epochs in our case) and find that they are in close agreement. Section~\ref{sec:discussion} summarizes our contributions and outlines directions for future work. 


\section{Chemical Reaction Networks}
\label{sec:reaction_networks}


\begin{definition}[\cite{feinberg}]

A Chemical Reaction Network (reaction network) is a triple $\SCR$ consisting of a finite set of \textit{species} $\mS = \{X_1, \dots, X_n \}$, a set of \textit{complexes} $\mC = \{ C_1, \dots, C_r \}$, and a set of \textit{reactions} $\mR = \{ R_1, \dots, R_r \}$. 
Each reaction describes an interaction between species given by:
\begin{equation} \notag
R_j : \sum_{i=1}^n \alpha_{ij} X_i \rightarrow \sum_{i=1}^n \beta_{ij} X_i
\ \text{ with } j = 1, \dots, r,
\end{equation}
where the left-hand side (\textit{reactant complex}) and the right-hand side (\textit{product complex}) are linear combinations of species.
For each reaction $R_j$, the nonnegative integer coefficients $ \alpha_{ij} $ and $ \beta_{ij} $ represent the \textit{stoichiometric coefficients} of species $ X_i $ in the reactant and product complexes, respectively.
\end{definition}

\begin{example} 
\label{ex:crnt} 

