In the following, we introduce two key concepts in reaction networks: catalytic and non-catalytic species, which will be frequently used in Section~\ref{sec:modules}.

\begin{definition}[\cite{feinberg}]

Let $\SCR$ be a reaction network. Suppose all reactions $R_1, \ldots, R_r \in \mR$, where each reaction is of the form 
\[
R_j: \sum_{i=1}^n \alpha_{ij} X_i \rightarrow \sum_{i=1}^n \beta_{ij} X_i.
\]
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] A species $X_i \in \mS$ is said to be \emph{catalytic} if the number of species $ X_i$ in the reactant and product complexes remains the same for every reaction. Specifically, $X_i$ is a catalytic species if and only if $\alpha_{ij} = \beta_{ij}$ for every $1 \leq i \leq r$.

\item[(b)] A species $X_i \in \mS$ is said to be a \emph{non-catalytic} species if it is not catalytic, that is, there exists a reaction $R_j$ such that $\alpha_{ij} \neq \beta_{ij}$. 
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}

In a reaction network, every species is either a catalytic species or a non-catalytic species. Under the mass-action system, catalytic species are those whose concentrations remain unchanged across the entire network, and therefore $\frac{d x_i}{dt} \equiv 0$; whereas for non-catalytic species, there exists at least one reaction that alters their concentration.
\end{remark}

\begin{example}

Consider the following reaction network:
\[
0 \to X_1 + X_2, \quad
X_1 + X_3 \to X_3
\]
In this example, the reaction network consists of three species, $\mS = \{ X_1, X_2, X_3 \}$. Among them, $X_1$ and $X_2$ are non-catalytic, while $X_3$ is a catalytic species.
\end{example}



\section{Support Vector Machines}\label{sec:support_vector_machines}


Support Vector Machines (SVMs) are supervised machine learning algorithms. SVMs have attracted widespread attention in machine learning, data mining and pattern recognition tasks. In particular, they have been found to be particularly useful in binary classification problems. 

SVM works on the principle of generating a surface that separates data into classes by maximizing the distance between itself and the data points. In particular, SVM assigns the data points labels by finding the \emph{optimal hyperplane}. This hyperplane corresponds to one that is at the maximum  distance from the closest point of any class. Given a new data point, it can be classified and given labels according to which side of the hyperplane it will be in feature space.

If the data cannot be separated by such a hyperplane, then the SVM finds the hyperplane as in the hard margin case with the caveat that a penalty is added each time a point crosses the margin. These SVMs are referred to as soft margin SVMs.

\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.75\linewidth]{ideal_hyperplane.png}
    \caption{Hard Margin SVM Decision Boundary}
    \label{fig:enter-label}
\end{figure}




\subsection{Hard-Margin SVM Formulation}

Given a set of data points (which are feature vectors), that belong to either of the two classes, one needs to decide to which of the two classes does a new data point belong to? More formally, given a dataset \( \{(x_i, y_i)\}_{i=1}^N \), where \( x_i \in \mathbb{R}^d \) are the feature vectors and \( y_i \in \{1, -1\} \) are the binary class labels, the goal is to find a weight vector \( \mathbf{w} \in \mathbb{R}^d \) 
such that it maximizes the distance of its nearest point for each class and bias \( b \in \mathbb{R} \) such that:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:max_margin}
    y_i (\mathbf{w} \cdot x_i + b) \geq 1 \quad \text{for all } i.
\end{equation}

The optimization problem for a hard-margin SVM can be defined as:
\begin{equation}
    \min_{\mathbf{w}, b} \frac{1}{2} \|\mathbf{w}\|^2,
\end{equation}
subject to the constraint that all training points satisfy:
\begin{equation}
    y_i (\mathbf{w} \cdot x_i + b) \geq 1.
\end{equation}

%Equation~\ref{eq:max_margin} ensures that we find a hyperplane that maximizes the distance to its nearest points.

\subsection{Iterative Training with Error-Driven Update}

In practice, we can use an iterative training algorithm that updates \( \mathbf{w} \) and \( b \) whenever a data point is misclassified. This approach is similar to a gradient descent method on hinge loss. Given a learning rate \( \eta > 0 \), we update the parameters as follows:

1. \textbf{Initialize} \( \mathbf{w} = 1 \) and \( b = 0 \).

2. \textbf{For each data point} \( (x_i, y_i) \):
    - Compute the decision function: \( f(x_i) = \mathbf{w} \cdot x_i + b \).
    % - If \( y_i f(x_i) < 1 \) (i.e., the margin constraint is violated), update the parameters:
    %   \begin{equation}
    %       \mathbf{w} \leftarrow \mathbf{w} + \eta y_i x_i,
    %   \end{equation}
    %   \begin{equation}
    %       b \leftarrow b + \eta y_i.
    %   \end{equation}
    The hinge loss for a single training example \((x_i, y_i)\) is defined as:
    \[
    \text{Loss}(w, b) = \max(0, 1 - y_i*f(x_i))
    \]

    To update the weights based on the hinge loss, we compute the gradients:

    \[
    \frac{\partial \text{Loss}}{\partial w} = 
    \begin{cases}
    - y_i x_i & \text{if } y_i (w \cdot x_i + b) < 1 \\
    0 & \text{otherwise}
    \end{cases}
    \]

    \[
    \frac{\partial \text{Loss}}{\partial b} = 
    \begin{cases}
    - y_i & \text{if } y_i (w \cdot x_i + b) < 1 \\
    0 & \text{otherwise}
    \end{cases}
    \]

    The weight update rules for gradient descent with learning rate \( \eta \) are as follows:

\begin{enumerate}



\item \textbf{If there is a misclassification or margin violation} (\( y_i (w \cdot x_i + b) < 1 \)):

\begin{eqnarray}\label{eq:mis_update}
\begin{split}
  w &\leftarrow w - \eta \left( \lambda w - y_i x_i \right) \\
   b &\leftarrow b + \eta y_i
\end{split}
\end{eqnarray}


\item \textbf{If there is no misclassification} (\( y_i (w \cdot x_i + b) \geq 1 \)):
    \begin{eqnarray}\label{eq:no_mis_update}
\begin{split}
       w &\leftarrow w - \eta \lambda w
       \\
       b &\leftarrow b
       \end{split}
\end{eqnarray}
Here \( \lambda \) is the regularization parameter.



\item  \textbf{Repeat} this process for multiple epochs until convergence or a set number of iterations is reached.

This update rule moves \( \mathbf{w} \) and \( b \) in the direction that reduces the error for misclassified points, adjusting the hyperplane iteratively. The learning rate \( \eta \) controls the step size for each update, impacting the convergence rate.

\end{enumerate}


\subsection{Decision Rule}

After training, the classification decision for a new data point \( x \) is given by:
\begin{equation}
    f(x) = \mathbf{w} \cdot x + b.
\end{equation}
The predicted class is then:
\[
\hat{y} = \begin{cases} 
      1 & \text{if } f(x) \geq 0, \\
      -1 & \text{otherwise}.
   \end{cases}
\]




This iterative approach, while not the standard quadratic programming formulation of SVM, provides a computationally efficient method for training hard-margin SVMs and is particularly useful in online or large-scale learning scenarios.









\section{Operation modules}\label{sec:modules}


In this section, we flesh out the reaction network scheme for the individual sub-modules that will used for implementing the feedforward and backpropagations step. Towards this, we note that in dealing with weights and biases, we will may encounter negative values, and need a mechanism to handle this. The next subsection on \emph{dual rail encoding} addresses this part.


\subsection{Dual rail encoding}


In physical setting, concentration of species is always non-negative. As data points and weights can be negative, we use the Dual Rail Encoding~\cite{vasic2020deep}. 

For any variable \( \zeta \in \mathbb{R} \), we take two species \( \zeta^+ \) and \( \zeta^- \) with non-negative concentrations \( \zeta^+(t) \geq 0 \) and \( \zeta^-(t) \geq 0 \). For all $t \geq 0$, the difference between \( \zeta^+(t) - \zeta^-(t) \) represents the real value \( \zeta \).


Here, we list some basic modules that will be used for in the implemention of the more  feedforward and backpropogation modules. Some of these have also been described in~\cite{vasic2020crn++}. In this description, we will denote by $[X(t)]$, the concentration corresponding to the species $X$ at time $t$.



\subsection{Addition module}

In order to add two numbers, we consider the following network:
\begin{eqnarray}\label{module_addition}
\begin{split}
A &\overset{1}\longrightarrow A + C \\
B &\overset{1}\longrightarrow B + C \\
C &\overset{1}\longrightarrow \emptyset
\end{split}
\end{eqnarray}


The concentrations of species $A$ and $B$ are added and the result is stored in the steady state concentration of $C$. The original input species $A$ and $B$ are catalysts and their concentrations are preserved. Let $[A] = [A(t)] = [A(0)]$ and $[B] = [B(t)] = [B(0)]$. Then the dynamics of  network~\ref{module_addition} is given by
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{d[C(t)]}{dt} = [A] + [B] - [C(t)]
\end{eqnarray}
%
The steady-state concentration of $[C(t)]$ (denoted by $[C^{ss}]$) is given by $[C^{ss}] = [A] + [B]$. 

\subsection{Multiplication module}

In order to multiply, we consider the following network:
\begin{eqnarray}\label{module_multiplication}
\begin{split}
A + B &\overset{1}\longrightarrow A + B + C \\
C &\overset{1}\longrightarrow\emptyset
\end{split}
\end{eqnarray}
%
Here, the concentrations of species $A$ and $B$ are multiplied and the result is stored in the steady-state concentration of $C$. Since $A$ and $B$ are catalysts, their concentrations are preserved. Let $[A] = [A(t)] = [A(0)]$ and $[B] = [B(t)] = [B(0)]$. Then the dynamics corresponding to network~\ref{module_multiplication} is given by

\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{d[C(t)]}{dt} = [A]\cdot [B] - [C(t)]
\end{eqnarray}
%
The steady-state concentration of $[C(t)]$ (denoted by $[C^{ss}]$) is given by $[C^{ss}] = [A]\cdot [B]$.  



\subsection{Comparison and Approximate majority}

Our module is designed to compare the concentrations of species and set the corresponding flags. This functionality is achieved using a \textit{comparison module} (CMP) and an \textit{approximate majority module} (AM), triggered in consecutive clock cycles.

\subsubsection*{Comparison Module (CMP)}


In the comparison (CMP) module, the inputs $X$ and $Y$ are assigned to flag species $X_{gtY}$ and $X_{ltY}$ in a normalized fashion. The network for implementing this is given by 
\begin{eqnarray}\label{module_comparison}
    \begin{split}
    X_{gtY} + Y &\overset{1}\longrightarrow X_{ltY} + Y, \\
    X_{ltY} + X &\overset{1}\longrightarrow X_{gtY} + X.
    \end{split}
\end{eqnarray}


In this network, the species $X$ and $Y$ are catalysts. We let $[X] = [X(0)]$ and $[Y] = [Y(0)]$. To normalize the values within the range $[0, 1]$, the initial concentrations of $X_{gtY}$ and $X_{ltY}$ are set such that $[X_{gtY}(0)] + [X_{ltY}(0)] = 1$. For example, if $[X(0)] = 60$ and $[Y(0)] = 40$, the flag species $X_{gtY}$ and $X_{ltY}$ will converge to $0.6$ and $0.4$, respectively. 
%This normalization preserves the original input values as they are catalytic species.

The dynamics corresponding to network~\ref{module_comparison} is given by
\begin{eqnarray}
\begin{split}
\frac{d[X_{gtY}(t)]}{dt} &= -[X_{gtY}(t)]\cdot [Y]  + [X_{ltY}(t)]\cdot [X] \\
\frac{d[X_{ltY}(t)]}{dt} &= [X_{gtY}(t)]\cdot [Y]  - [X_{ltY}(t)]\cdot [X]
\end{split}
\end{eqnarray}
%
At steady-state, we get
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{[X_{gtY}^{ss}]}{[X_{ltY}^{ss}]} = \frac{[X]}{[Y]}
\end{eqnarray}
Note that $[X_{ltY}^{ss}] + [X_{gtY}^{ss}]=1$. This implies that 
\begin{eqnarray}
\begin{split}
[X_{gtY}^{ss}] &= \frac{[X]}{[X] + [Y]}\\
[X_{ltY}^{ss}] &=\frac{[Y]}{[X] + [Y]}. 
\end{split}
\end{eqnarray}


\subsubsection*{Approximate Majority module (AMP)}\label{sec:approximate_majority}

In the approximate Majority Module (AM) module~\cite{cardelli2012cell}, molecules of species possessing lower population  are converted into molecules of species possessing higher population through the following network:
\begin{eqnarray}\label{module_approximate_majority}
\begin{split}
    X_{gtY} + X_{ltY} &\overset{1}\longrightarrow X_{ltY} + B, \\
    B + X_{ltY} &\overset{1}\longrightarrow X_{ltY} + X_{ltY}, \\
    X_{ltY} + X_{gtY} &\overset{1}\longrightarrow X_{gtY} + B, \\
    B + X_{gtY} &\overset{1}\longrightarrow X_{gtY} + X_{gtY}.
\end{split}
\end{eqnarray}

The dynamical system corresponding to the module~\ref{module_approximate_majority} is given by 

\begin{eqnarray}
\begin{split}
\frac{d[X_{gtY}(t)]}{dt} &= -[X_{gtY}(t)]\cdot [X_{ltY}(t)]   + [B(t)]\cdot [X_{gtY}(t)] \\
\frac{d[X_{ltY}(t)]}{dt} &= -[X_{gtY}(t)]\cdot [X_{ltY}(t)]   + [B(t)]\cdot [X_{ltY}(t)]\\
\frac{d[B(t)]}{dt} &= -[B(t)]\cdot [X_{ltY}(t)]   - [B(t)]\cdot [X_{gtY}(t)] + 2[X_{gtY}(t)]\cdot [X_{ltY}(t)]
\end{split}
\end{eqnarray}

Assuming the normalization step in the comparison module (CMP) has already been executed, the species $(X_{gtY}, X_{ltY}, B)$ will converge to the state $(1, 0, 0)$ if $X_{gtY}(0) > X_{ltY}(0)$, and to $(0, 1, 0)$ if $X_{gtY}(0) < X_{ltY}(0)$. [For a proof of this fact, please refer~\cite{vasic2020crn++}.]

As it will turn out, in Section~\ref{sec:learning} $X_{gtY}$ will serve as a catalyst in reactions that occur when $X > Y$, while $X_{ltY}$ plays the same role for the opposite condition.

\subsection{Loading module}

The loading module copies the concentration of $A$ into $B$ using the following network:
\begin{equation}\label{module_loading}
\begin{split}
A &\rightarrow A + B \\
B &\rightarrow \emptyset
\end{split}
\end{equation}
%
In this network, the species $A$ is a catalyst. Let $[A] = [A(0)]$.
Then the dynamical system corresponding to~\ref{module_loading} is given by 
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{d[B]}{dt} = [A] - [B]
\end{eqnarray}
%
At steady state, we get
$[B^{ss}] = [A]$.


\subsection{Subtraction Module}
The subtraction module computes $C := \max(0, A - B)$. The reaction network corresponding to this is given by:
\begin{equation}\label{module_subtraction}
\begin{split}
A &\rightarrow A + C \\
B &\rightarrow B + H \\
C &\rightarrow \emptyset\\
C + H &\rightarrow \emptyset\\
\end{split}
\end{equation}
%
The species $A$ and $B$ are catalysts. Let $[A] = [A(0)]$ and $[B] = [B(0)]$. Then the dynamics corresponding to  network~\ref{module_subtraction} is given by
\begin{eqnarray}
\begin{split}
\frac{d[C(t)]}{dt} &= [A] - [C(t)][H(t)]  - [C(t)]\\
\frac{d[H(t)]}{dt} &= [B]   - [C(t)][H(t)] 
\end{split}
\end{eqnarray}
%
This implies that at steady-state $[C^{ss}] = [A] - [B]$. If $[A] > [B]$, then $C=[A] - [B]$. If $[A] \leq [B]$, then $[C]= 0$.




\subsection{Oscillation module with doping}

Oscillating molecules \cite{lachmann1995computationally,BALLARINI20093,FAN20227} are used for sequential execution of reactions in a network. In particular, the concentrations of these molecules oscillate such that when the concentration of a certain species is positive, the concentrations of the remaining species are zero. For example, consider the following network
%We use these species as catalysts in our network. This ensures we can control occurrence of our reactions without any external intervention.

\[
\begin{aligned}
    O_1 + O_2 &\rightarrow 2O_2 \\
    % D_1 + O_n &\rightarrow O_1
\end{aligned}
\qquad
\begin{aligned}
    O_i + O_{i+1} &\rightarrow 2O_{i+1} \\
    % D_{i} + O_{i-1} &\rightarrow O_{i}
\end{aligned}
\qquad
\begin{aligned}
    O_n + O_1 &\rightarrow 2O_1  \\
    % D_n + O_{n-1} &\rightarrow O_n
\end{aligned}
\]



The network contains $n$ oscillating species, denoted by $O_{i}$. If a reaction contains $O_{i}$ and another reaction contains $O_{j}$, such that $i < j$, then the reaction containing the oscillation molecule $O_{i}$ will be executed before the reaction containing the oscillation molecule $O_{j}$.

However, these oscillations eventually die out when one of the species becomes extinct, meaning the oscillations are not perpetual. To make them perpetual, Ballarini et.al \cite{BALLARINI20093}. introduce the notion of a \textit{doping reaction} for each species. A doping reaction is defined as one that generates a molecule of a species \( X \in \{1, 2, \dots, n\} \) by consuming a molecule of a doping substance and another species. 

In practical terms, the chemical equations representing the \( n \)-way oscillator with doping are as follows:

\[
\begin{aligned}
    O_1 + O_2 &\rightarrow 2O_2 \\
    D_1 + O_n &\rightarrow O_1
\end{aligned}
\qquad
\begin{aligned}
    O_i + O_{i+1} &\rightarrow 2O_{i+1} \\
    D_{i} + O_{i-1} &\rightarrow O_{i}
\end{aligned}
\qquad
\begin{aligned}
    O_n + O_1 &\rightarrow 2O_1  \\
    D_n + O_{n-1} &\rightarrow O_n
\end{aligned}
\]

The presence of doping species ensures that the oscillator is perpetual.



\section{Encoding the  feedforward module of SVM}\label{sec:feedforward}


%Change it to have the same oscillating molecule.

Given an $n$-dimensional input vector $X$, and its associated label $Y$, we can use our network to determine whether it has been classified correctly or not (i.e. the associated label Y is correct or not). We can do this by computing
\begin{eqnarray}
\begin{split}
Y \times ( W \cdot X  + b ) \\
\end{split}
\end{eqnarray}

and comparing it with 1.

We do this in a sequence of five steps:


\textbf{Step 1:}

We first compute the dot product of $W$ and $X$. We do so by multiplying W and X pair-wise and storing the the product in $WX$ (for every $i$, the product of $W_{i}$ and $X_{i}$ is stored in $WX_{i}$). The network below computes $WX_{i} = W_{i} \times X_{i}$. 

\begin{eqnarray}\label{eq:W_dot_X}
\begin{split}
W_1 + X_1 + O_1  &\overset{1}\longrightarrow W_1 + X_1 + WX_1 + O_1 \\
WX_1 + O_1 &\overset{1}\longrightarrow O_1 \\
%W_2 + X_2 + O_1  %&\rightarrow W_2 + X_2 + %WX_2 + O_1 \\
%WX_2 + O_1 &\rightarrow O_1 %\\
\vdots & \\
W_N + X_N + O_1  &\overset{1}\longrightarrow W_N + X_N + WX_N + O_1 \\
WX_N + O_1 &\overset{1}\longrightarrow O_1 
\end{split}
\end{eqnarray}

The species $W_i,X_i,O_i$ are catalysts in this network. Let $[W_i] = [W_i(0)], [X_i] = [X_i(0)]$ and $[O_i] = [O_i(0)]$.

The dynamical system corresponding to Equation~\ref{eq:W_dot_X} is given by
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{d[WX_i]}{dt} = [W_i] [X_i] [O_1] - [WX_i] [O_1]
\end{eqnarray}

At steady state, we get $[{WX_i}^{ss}] = [W_i][X_i]$ as required.



\textbf{Step 2:}

 We then sum all $WX_{i}$ and add the bias $b$ and store the this sum in a new species, called $P$. The network below computes the following quantity: $P = ( \sum _{i=1}^N WX_{i} ) + b$

\begin{eqnarray}\label{eq:step_2}
\begin{split}
WX_1 + O_1 &\overset{1}\longrightarrow WX_1 + P + O_1 \\
%WX_2 + O_1 &\rightarrow %WX_2 + P + O_1 \\
\vdots & \\
WX_N + O_1 &\overset{1}\longrightarrow WX_N + P + O_1 \\
% \beta + O_1 &\rightarrow \beta + P + O_1 \\
b + O_1 &\overset{1}\longrightarrow b + P + O_1 \\
P + O_1 &\overset{1}\longrightarrow O_1
\end{split}
\end{eqnarray}


The dynamical system corresponding to the network~\ref{eq:step_2} is given by
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{d[P]}{dt} = \displaystyle\sum_i [W_i] [X_i] [O_1]  + [b][O_1] - [P][O_1]
\end{eqnarray}

At steady state, we get $[P^{ss}] =\displaystyle\sum_i [W_i] [X_i] + [b]$.

\textbf{Step 3:}

We first multiply $P$ with $Y$ and store the result in $Q$.
\begin{eqnarray}\label{eq:step_3}
    Q = Y \times P
\end{eqnarray}
All reactions containing $O_{1}$ would be executed parallely and before other reactions. At steady state, we would have computed     
\begin{eqnarray}
Y \times ( W \cdot X  + b ) 
\end{eqnarray}
and stored the value in $Q$.


Now, we compare the value in $Q$ with $1$. If the associated label is correct, then concentration of $Q$ will be greater than $1$ and a flag
will be set, in the form of concentration of some species converging
to $1$. We divide this comparison process into two steps, Step $4$ (which
compares) and Step $5$ (which sets the flag). As these steps need to
happen sequentially, we use oscillating species. Oscillation molecule $O_2$ is used in Step $4$, whereas $O_3$ is used in Step $5$. This ensures reactions of Step $4$ finish
before Step $5$ reactions begin.
\begin{eqnarray}\label{eq:step_3}
\begin{split}
P + Y + O_1 &\overset{1}\longrightarrow P + Y + Q + O_1 \\
Q + O_1 &\overset{1}\longrightarrow O_1
\end{split}
\end{eqnarray}

The dynamical system corresponding to network~\ref{eq:step_3} is 
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{d[Q]}{dt} = [P][Y][O_1] - [Q][O_1]
\end{eqnarray}

At steady state, we get $[Q^{ss}] = [P]\cdot [Y]$.


\textbf{Step 4:}

In Step $4$, we introduce the following new species: $K$, $K_{ltQ}$ and $K_{gtQ}$. We will compare the concentration of $Q$ with the concentration of $K$ (which is set to $1$). $K_{ltQ}$ and $K_{gtQ}$ are flag species such that the sum of their initial concentrations is $1$. 

The network that accomplishes this is given by 
\begin{eqnarray}\label{eq:step_4}
\begin{split}
Q + K_{gtQ} + O_2 &\overset{1}\longrightarrow K_{ltQ} + Q + O_2 \\
K_{ltQ} + K + O_2 &\overset{1}\longrightarrow K_{gtQ} + K + O_2 
\end{split}
\end{eqnarray}


The dynamical system corresponding to network~\ref{eq:step_4} is given by 
\begin{eqnarray}
\begin{split}
\frac{[K_{ltQ}]}{dt} &= [Q][K_{gtQ}][O_2] - [K_{ltQ}][K][O_2] \\
\frac{[K_{gtQ}]}{dt} &= -[Q][K_{gtQ}][O_2] + [K_{ltQ}][K][O_2] 
\end{split}
\end{eqnarray}
%
Consequently, steady-state concentrations of $K_{ltQ}$ and $K_{gtQ}$ will be set as follows:
    \begin{eqnarray}
        [K_{ltQ}^{ss}] = \frac{[Q]}{[Q]+[K]} 
    \end{eqnarray}
    \begin{eqnarray}
                [K_{gtQ}^{ss}] = \frac{[K]}{[Q]+[K]} 
\end{eqnarray}
%
where $[K] = [K(0)]$ and $[Q] = [Q(0)]$.

\textbf{Step 5:}

By end of Step $4$, we have compared $Q$ and $K$, and we have set the values of $K_{ltQ}$ and $K_{gtQ}$ as shown above.

In Step $5$, approximate majority algorithm (see Section~\ref{sec:approximate_majority}) is used to detect which flag species is in majority. In particular, we try to set the flags, such that if $Q >K$, then $K_{ltQ}$ converges to $1$ and $K_{gtQ}$ converges to $0$. If $K > Q$, then vice versa happens. The network corresponding to this is given by

\begin{eqnarray}\label{eq:step_5}
\begin{split}
K_{gtQ} + K_{ltQ} + O_3 &\overset{1}\longrightarrow K_{ltQ} + B + O_3 \\
B + K_{ltQ} + O_3 &\overset{1}\longrightarrow K_{ltQ} + K_{ltQ} + O_3 \\
K_{ltQ} + K_{gtQ} + O_3 &\overset{1}\longrightarrow K_{gtQ} + B + O_3 \\
B + K_{gtQ} + O_3 &\overset{1}\longrightarrow K_{gtQ} + K_{gtQ} + O_3 
\end{split}
\end{eqnarray}



The dynamical system corresponding to network~\ref{eq:step_5} is given by
\begin{eqnarray}
\begin{split}
\frac{[K_{ltQ}]}{dt} &= -[K_{gtQ}][K_{ltQ}][O_3] + [B][K_{ltQ}][O_3] \\
\frac{[dQ]}{dt} &= -[K_{gtQ}][K_{ltQ}][O_3] + [B][K_{gtQ}][O_3] \\
\frac{[dB]}{dt} &= [K_{gtQ}][K_{ltQ}][O_3] - [B][K_{gtQ}][O_3] + [K_{ltQ}][K_{gtQ}][O_3] - [B][K_{gtQ}][O_3]
\end{split}
\end{eqnarray}

The analysis of the steady states follows from the discussion in subsection~\ref{sec:approximate_majority}.



After Step $5$, if $K_{ltQ}$ converges to 1 and $K_{gtQ}$ converges to 0, that means our associated label was correct. If the vice versa happens, that implies that associated label was wrong.



