\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}

We have shown how to implement hard margin support vector machines using reaction networks. Our method involves implementing the feedforward and backpropagation module of the support vector machine. Towards this, we have used various sub-modules to accomplish this. In particular, these include the addition module, the subtraction module, the multiplication module, the comparison module and the approximate majority module. To handle sequential execution of reactions, we use time-scale separation, which is handled by the oscillation module coupled with doping. To handle the possibility of negative weights and biases, we make use of the dual rail encoding.

Our work intersects with several other works that have used reaction networks to implement machine learning algorithms. In particular, these include the use of reaction networks to model neural networks~\cite{anderson2021reaction}, the use of reaction networks to implement boltzmann machines~\cite{poole2017chemical,poole2025autonomous}  and also to compute maximum likelihood estimates~\cite{gopalkrishnan2016scheme}.

Our works raises several avenues for future work and investigation. 

\textbf{Question:} Can you compute the complexity of this implementation (in terms of number of molecules, number of reactions and rate of convergence for various steps as described in~\cite{anderson2025chemical})? 

Another possibility is:


\textbf{Question:} Does our setup satisfy the following optimal criteria of computation as laid out in~\cite{anderson2021reaction}? 
In particular, assume that our reaction network can be represented as a dynamical system of the form $\dot x(t) = f(y,x(t))$ with $x(t) \in \R^n_{\ge 0}$. 


\begin{enumerate}
    \item Is our computation exponentially reliable? In other words, does there exists a $\zeta:\R^p_{>0} \to \R_{>0}$ such that 
\begin{equation*}
   \hspace{1.5in} |x(t) - \xi(y)| \le |x(0) - \xi(y)|e^{-\zeta(y) t}\hspace{1.6in},
\end{equation*}
where $\displaystyle\lim_{t\to\infty}x(t)=\xi(y)$.
    \item Do the solutions of our systems converge to a compact set containing the equilibrium in finite time, In other words, does there exists compact set $K \subset \R^n_{\ge 0}$ and a $T:\R^p_{>0} \to \R_{>0}$ such that $x(t) \in K$ for any $t \ge T(y)$ and $x(0)\in\R^n_{\ge 0}$?
\end{enumerate}



Another possibility is to see how to extend our framework to \emph{soft margin support vector machines}? Can we modify our framework to incorporate the \emph{kernel trick} to classify nonlinear data?


