\begin{abstract}
We propose a reformulation of the streaming dynamic mode decomposition method that requires maintaining a single orthonormal basis, thereby reducing computational redundancy. The proposed efficient streaming dynamic mode decomposition method results in a constant-factor reduction in computational complexity and memory storage requirements. Numerical experiments on representative canonical dynamical systems show that the enhanced computational efficiency does not compromise the accuracy of the proposed method.

% Streaming dynamic mode decomposition (sDMD) is a real-time algorithm that performs dynamic mode decomposition (DMD) on, iteratively, on a stream of data. It does this by maintaining two low-dimensional orthonormal bases of the ambient state space. We propose a simplified alternative to sDMD that reduces computational redundancy by maintaining only a single orthonormal basis. We demonstrate that our method achieves comparable accuracy to standard sDMD—benchmarked against batch DMD—when applied to representative canonical systems.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Dynamic mode decomposition, Koopman operator, modal analysis, nonlinear dynamical systems, stream processing.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

