\section{Adding an Artificial Agent and Global Uniqueness}
\label{global-uniq}

In this section we use a different procedure to select the alternative if many are made and show that we obtain global uniqueness for the case of an odd number of agents.

\subsection{Artificial Agent and the New Procedure }
As we have seen in the previous section, when the number of agents is even, the equilibrium is generally not unique. To ensure that the number of agents is always odd, we introduce a preliminary stage in which an artificial agent is added to the system. This artificial agent, referred to as the \emph{AI voter}, participates in the voting process only when the number of real agents is even. The AI voter can be thought of as a machine that casts an additional vote according to the following procedure.

\begin{description}
    \item[Stage 0: ] If $N$ is odd, proceed as before. If $N$ is even, draw an AI voter with peak $\theta_{AI}$ in $[-A, A]$. This AI will always vote. In total, with the AI there are $N+1$ agents.
\end{description}

Then, the adjusted procedure is given as follows: 

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Each agent decides whether he will propose a policy or be placed into the pool of voters. The set of proposers will be denoted by $\CP$ and the set of voters by $\CV$.

    \item Each proposer $p \in \CP$ decides on a proposal $x_p \in [-A,A]$ he wants to put forward.

    \item Among all proposers in $\CP$, two are selected uniformly at random and their proposal are submitted to a vote. Only the agents in the voter pool $\CV$ are allowed to participate in the voting process. The proposal that loses the vote, determined by majority rule, is discarded. Let $\mathcal{D}$ denote the set of agents whose proposals have been discarded. The winning proposal $x_w$ is then voted against another proposal, selected uniformly at random from the remaining proposals in $\CP \setminus (\mathcal{D} \cup \{w\})$. This elimination procedure continues until only one proposal remains. The proposal made by agent $p \in \CP \setminus \mathcal{D}$ that survives all rounds is implemented. Ties are resolved through fair randomization.
\end{enumerate}

Hence, we have now a procedure to select the winning alternative. We can now leverage the result from \cite{gersbach2025votingrandomproposersrounds} who have shown that such a procedure yields the Condorcet Winner among the proposals put forward by the group of proposal-makers.\footnote{Applying the procedure by \cite{gersbach2025votingrandomproposersrounds} requires an adjustment to a finite number of agents and some technical assumptions. We assume that these conditions are fulfilled in this section. }

\subsection{Uniqueness for Model with AI voter}

We show that in this model there is a unique equilibrium. Remember that in this model, the number of agents is always odd, as if it is even a new AI voter is added to make it odd. 

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:model_iii_uniqueness}
    Let the number of agents $N$ be odd. The scenario in which the median agent $m$ opts for submitting the proposal $\theta_m$ and all other agents opt to vote is the unique equilibrium.
\end{theorem}

The proof, that can be found in the Appendix, is made of three steps. First, we show that the Condorcet winner of the set $\CP$ is always implemented if it exists.  Second, we show the existence of an equilibrium in our model. In the final step, we show that the equilibrium we found in the second step is unique.

\section{Elections}
\label{elections}

The procedure can be applied in the same way for an election to an office. Agents decide whether to apply for proposal-making or voting. Now a proposal is a candidate for office. An agent opting for proposal-making may propose himself/herself for the office or some other agents. The remaining agents then vote. Our analysis and results can be readily transferred to such a set-up. The only difference is that the set of possible alternatives is now constrained to the set of agents $N$ and is a discrete set of alternatives. Note that a candidate who is proposed by another agent can vote.\footnote{Again, one can add an AA to achieve an odd number of participants, but the AA is only active in voting. Yet, there are couple of subtle issues with an AA agent participating in elections, which we will spell out in future versions of the paper.}








\section{Discussion and Outlook}
\label{conclusion}

We demonstrate that the Propose-or-Vote mechanism efficiently implements the socially optimal alternative in a simple setting and that the Propose-or-Vote (PoV) procedure selects the Condorcet winner in a single voting round.
We have used a simple framework in which the distribution of peaks of the individual preferences is public knowledge and the peaks are public knowledge. 

Numerous extensions should and will be taken up in future research. Those extensions range from asymmetric information to more complex arrangements of peaks of agents and utility functions. 

