        \item 
            Assume that $\theta_{mv} < \theta_{p_1} < \theta_{p_2}$. This situation is symmetric to the case $(i)$ and thus the same arguments apply.
    \end{enumerate}

\subsection{Proof of Theorem 2}

We start by showing that the Condorcet winner is always implemented, if it exists.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:model_iii_condorcet_winner}
    Let $\CP$ be the set of proposers, $\CV$ the set of voters. Assume that there exists a Condorcet winner in $\CP$ w.r.t. $\CV$, i.e. a proposer $p \in \CP$ whose proposal would win any one-on-one vote against any other proposal in $\CP$. Then $p$ is implemented.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
    Since voters vote truthfully in each round of the procedure, and since the proposal of $p$ wins any one-on-one voting round against any other proposal made by agents in $\CP$, it will eventually be selected as one of the contenders in some round. From that point on, it will continue to win every subsequent voting round. Therefore, $p$ will be the last remaining proposal and will be implemented at the end of the procedure.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}[Proof of \Cref{thm:model_iii_uniqueness}]
    We will first show that the presented equilibrium candidate is indeed an equilibrium, and then prove that it is the unique equilibrium.
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
        \item \textit{Existence: } 
            Since there is only one proposal made, any deviation from the current strategy by an agent leads to a scenario where at most one voting round is needed. Moreover, if some of the voter deviates by making a proposal, there is an odd number of voters, and the AI abstains. Thus, we are in the exact same situation as in the proof of Theorem \ref{theorem:vop-theorem} and, therefore, the same arguments apply.

        \item \textit{Uniqueness: }
            Let there be an equilibrium consisting of voters $\mathcal{V}^*$ and proposers with proposals $\mathcal{P}^*$. Since AI votes iff there is a draw and abstains otherwise, there always exists a Condorcet winner, since the number of voting agents $\mathcal{V}^*$ including potentially AI is odd. Thus, by \Cref{prop:model_iii_condorcet_winner}, the proposal $p_w \coloneqq \argmin_{p \in \mathcal{P}^*} |x_p - \theta_{mv}|$ closest to the median voter $\theta_{mv}$ wins the vote and is implemented. Therefore, there is exactly one proposal made, that is, $|\mathcal{P}^*|=1$. 

            This can be easily proven by contradiction. If there is more than one proposal, the agents who made a proposal which is not implemented will obtain the same utility by switching to voting or even improve its utility through the shift of the median voter's type $\theta_{mv}$ towards its own type. Thus, by the tie-breaking rule, they will decide to vote. If there is no proposal, any agent has the incentive to change its strategy to propose its own peak as it will be implemented being the only proposal.

            Next, since there is only one proposal, it is always optimal for the proposal-making agent to propose its own peak. Assume by contradiction that the median agent $\theta_m$ is not making a proposal, but instead another agent of type $\theta_p \neq \theta_m$. We can assume w.l.o.g. that $\theta_p < \theta_m$, the case $\theta_p > \theta_m$ is symmetric. The agent with type $\theta_{m+1}$ right of the median agent (not the median voter) can improve its utility by proposing the median agent type $\theta_m$. If $\theta_{m+1}$ does so, the median voter coincides with the median agent since there is a proposal-making agent to the left and right of it. Thus, $x_{m+1}=\theta_m$ wins the vote and is implemented. Since 
            \begin{equation*}
                \mathbb{E}(u_{\theta_{m+1}, \theta_m})=-(\theta_m - \theta_{m+1})^2> -(\theta_p- \theta_{m+1})^2= \mathbb{E}(u_{\theta_{m+1}, v}),
            \end{equation*}
            this can not be an equilibrium. Hence, the equilibrium found above is the unique equilibrium.
    \end{enumerate}
\end{proof}



