If $\theta=\frac{\pi}{2}$, then either $\alpha=\frac{\pi}{2}$ or $\beta=\frac{\pi}{2}$. If $\alpha=\frac{\pi}{2}$. Then, $\beta>\frac{\pi}{2}$ and $\beta-\theta=\beta-\frac{\pi}{2}>0$ must also be a nonzero allowed angle that is less than $\pi$, since that is where the line through the origin at angle $\beta$ is mapped under the rotation by $-\theta$. Therefore, either $\beta-\frac{\pi}{2}=\beta$ which is not true, or $\beta-\frac{\pi}{2}=\alpha=\frac{\pi}{2}$ so $\beta=\pi$ which is also not the case. If $\beta=\frac{\pi}{2}$, then, $\alpha<\frac{\pi}{2}$, so $alpha$ is mapped to $\alpha+\theta=\alpha+\frac{\pi}{2}<\pi$ which must be another allowed angle less than $\pi$ so either $\alpha+\frac{\pi}{2}=\alpha$ which is not true, or $\alpha+\frac{\pi}{2}=\beta=\frac{\pi}{2}$ and $\alpha=0$ which is also not the case. Therefore, $\theta\neq \frac{\pi}{2}$ so $\theta<\frac{\pi}{2}$.

