We can describe the behavior of the cost of anarchy at the extremes of system size and impact decay. The cost of anarchy increases as $N$ increases, but has a finite limit, \[\lim_{N\uparrow\infty} \mathsf{CoA}_N = \frac{\beta T}{2(\beta T+1)^2}.\]
Interestingly, as illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:incr.sys.imp.cost}, the cost of anarchy is maximized at an intermediate value of $\beta$. For large $N$ this maximum occurs near $\beta\approx \frac{1}{T}$ and amounts to roughly $12.5\%$. A partial explanation is provided by the limits,
\[\lim_{\beta\downarrow0} \mathsf{CoA}_N = \lim_{\beta\uparrow\infty} \mathsf{CoA}_N  = 0.\]
For $\beta\to0$, there is essentially no resilience and the transient impact behaves like permanent impact. Hence, all liquidation strategies have the same cumulative (across agents) impact cost and the cost of anarchy tends to zero. For $\beta\to\infty$, the behavior is akin to temporary price impact with no block costs. Agents unwind their inventory at a constant rate in equilibrium, and that is also the central planner's limiting strategy. The impact costs tend to zero in either case, $\lim_{\beta\uparrow\infty}\mathsf{PIC}_N(x) =\lim_{\beta\uparrow\infty}\mathsf{PIC}_1(x)=0$. They do so at the same rate, resulting in $\lim_{\beta\uparrow\infty} \mathsf{CoA}_N =0$. 

\subsection{Cost of Predation}\label{se:cost.predation}

