Next we state a technical lemma we will use to prove the discreteness of the extremal rays later, following \cite{CHLX23}.

\begin{lemma}[cf. {\cite[Proposition 8.4.3]{CHLX23}}]\label{lemma:discreterays}
Let $X$ be a normal projective variety, and let $\omega$ be an $\R$-Cartier divisor on $X$. Let $A_1,\dots, A_{\rho-1}$ be $\R$-Cartier divisors on $X$ such that $\omega, A_1,\dots, A_{\rho-1}$ form a basis for $N^1(X)_\R$. Assume that $\{C_j\}_{j=1}^\infty$ is a sequence of $1$-cycles on $X$ such that for each $k = 1, 2,\dots, \rho-1$, $(A_k\cdot C_j)$ is independent of $j$, and $(\omega \cdot C_j)$ is bounded for all $j$. Then there exists an $\omega'\in N^1(X)$ such that $\omega', A_1,\dots,A_{\rho-1}$ form a basis for $N^1(X)_\R$, and after replacing $\{C_j\}$ by a subsequence, $(\omega' \cdot C_j)$ is independent of $j$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
    We may write $\omega = \sum_{i=1}^n r_iD_i$ where $r_i\in \R$ and $D_i$ are $\Q$-Cartier divisors for all $i=1,2,\dots n$. We may write \[
    D_i  \equiv \sum_{k=1}^{\rho-1} a_{i,k}A_k + b_i\omega.
