    Let $\delta_i\in \R$ such that $1 + \sum_{i=1}^n b_i \delta_i \neq 0$ and $r_i' := r_i + \delta_i\in \Q$. Let $\omega' = \sum_{i=1}^n r_i'D_i$, then $\omega'$ is  a $\Q$-Cartier $\Q$-divisor, and we have 
    \[
    \omega' = \sum_{i=1}^n r_i'D_i \equiv\left( 1+ \sum_{i=1}^n b_i\delta_i\right)\omega + \sum_{k=1}^{\rho-1}\left(\sum_{i = 1}^n \delta_i a_{i,k} \right)A_{k}.
    \] 
    Then $\omega,A_1,\dots, A_{\rho-1}$ form a basis of $N^1(X)_\R$. Let
    \[
    \alpha := \sum_{k=1}^{\rho-1}\left(\sum_{i = 1}^n \delta_i a_{i,k} \right)(A_{k}\cdot C_j), \quad \beta := 1+ \sum_{i=1}^n b_i\delta_i
    \]
    be two constants. Note that $\alpha$ is independent of $j$. Then \[
    (\omega'\cdot C_j) = \beta(\omega\cdot C_j) + \alpha.\]
    Since $(\omega\cdot C_j)$ is bounded for all $j$, it follows that $ (\omega' \cdot C_j) $ is also bounded. Since $\omega'$ is $\Q$-Cartier, the values of $(\omega' \cdot C_j)$ belong to a finite set. By replacing $ \{C_j\} $ with a suitable subsequence we may conclude.
\end{proof}
