Several authors have reported that fluctuations in the empirical first digit values can also occur, although the typically asymmetric distribution of digits. This observation implies deviations between the data and the frequency values predicted by the $NBL$. Evidence corroborating these observations comes from seismic activity and cognition experiments \cite{diaz2015,gauvrit2017}. This issue motivated several authors to propose a $NBL$ generalization. In this framework, for instance, one may highlight the theoretical description introduced by Pietronero \textit{et al.} \cite{pietronero2001}. According to this author, assuming a probability distribution $P_{\alpha}(d)$ (where d is the digit(s), $D$ represents an arbitrary digit(s) and $\alpha$ is a constant exponent related to the scale proportion), one may write:

%\begin{linenomath}
\begin{equation}
 P_{\alpha}(d)  =  \int_{d}^{d+1} D^{-\alpha} dD , \label{eq-pn}
\end{equation}
%\end{linenomath}
or by the differential equation:
%\begin{linenomath}
\begin{equation}
 \frac{dP_{\alpha}(D)}{dD}  =  D^{-\alpha}.
 \label{eq-pn-diff}
\end{equation}
%\end{linenomath}

Solving eq. (\ref{eq-pn-diff}) results in an $\alpha$-logarithm:
%\begin{linenomath}
\begin{eqnarray}
 P_{\alpha}(d) & = & \frac{1}{1-\alpha}\left[ \left(d+1\right)^{(1-\alpha)} - d^{(1-\alpha)}\right] \\
   & = & d^{(1-\alpha)} \ \ln_{\alpha}\left( \frac{d+1}{d} \right).
 \label{eq-pn-sol}
\end{eqnarray}
%\end{linenomath}

According to eq. (\ref{eq-pn-sol}), defined as generalized $NBL$ $(gNBL)$, more frequent first digits than expected by $NBL$ implies $\alpha>1$, while $\alpha<1$ means a first digit frequency below the predicted percentage. As expected, when $\alpha=1$ $NBL$ is fully recovered. Taking $n=d_{1}$,  equation (\ref{eq-pn-sol}) rewritten as:

%{\color{black}
%Verificar linha com estouro de largura
%\begin{linenomath}
\begin{equation}
 P_{\alpha}( d_{1} )  =  d_{1}^{1-\alpha} \ln_{\alpha}\left( \frac{d_{1}+1}{d_{1}}  \right),  \label{gbenford}
\end{equation}
%\end{linenomath}

From the approach developed by Pietronero \textit{et al.} \cite{pietronero2001}, it is also possible to obtain expressions for the second digit:
%\begin{linenomath}
\begin{equation}
  P_{\alpha}( d_{2} )  =  \sum_{d_{1}=1}^{9} (d_{1}d_{2})^{1-\alpha} \ln_{\alpha}\left( \frac{d_{1}d_{2}+1}{d_{1}d_{2}}  \right)  .  \label{gbenford-2c}
\end{equation}
%\end{linenomath}

Finally, the generalized Benford's Probability for the first two digits is presented as:
%\begin{linenomath}
\begin{equation}
 P_{\alpha}(d_{1}d_{2})  =  \displaystyle  \frac{ \left[ \left( d_{1}d_{2}+1 \right)^{1-\alpha}  - \left( d_{1}d_{2} \right)^{1-\alpha}     \right]  }{1-\alpha}, \ \ \ d_{1}d_{2} \in \lbrace 10,11,12,...,99 \rbrace , % \nonumber
\end{equation}
%\end{linenomath}
normalized for each $\alpha$ value.



